## Why This Is Asked
Tests design of robust, multi-tenant cache invalidation with Redis and Memcached, covering versioning, streams, stampede protection, and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Versioned per-tenant keys
- Redis Streams or Pub/Sub for invalidation
- Stampede protection via short TTLs and mutexes
- Cross-service invalidation consistency
- Observability and test plans

## Code Example
```javascript
function publishInvalidation(tenantId, itemId, ver){
  redis.xadd(`cache_invalidate:tenant:${tenantId}`, '*', 'item', itemId, 'ver', ver);
}
async function readItem(tenantId, itemId){
  const v = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`);
  const cached = await redis.get(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`);
  if (cached && v === latestVersionFromStore(itemId)){
    return JSON.parse(cached);
  } else {
    const fresh = await fetchFromStore(tenantId, itemId);
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:data`, JSON.stringify(fresh));
    await redis.set(`tenant:${tenantId}:item:${itemId}:v`, latestVersionFromStore(itemId));
    return fresh;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt to Memcached-only environments?
- How would you measure correctness and latency impact?
- How would you handle cascading invalidations across services?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design robust cache invalidation with multiple caches, versioned keys, and a practical flow that avoids stampede.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern with per-key versioning
- TTL-based data keys and version keys in Redis
- Fallback to Memcached and consistency between layers
- SWR to prevent repeated DB hits during refresh

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal get with versioned cache
async function getProduct(id) {
  let ver = await redis.get(`product:ver:${id}`);
  if (!ver) ver = '1';
  let data = await redis.get(`product:data:${id}:${ver}`);
  if (data) return JSON.parse(data);
  const row = await db.query('SELECT * FROM products WHERE id=?', [id]);
  await redis.set(`product:data:${id}:${ver}`, JSON.stringify(row), 'EX', 60);
  return row;
}

// Minimal update with invalidation
async function updateProduct(id, updates) {
  await db.run('UPDATE products SET ... WHERE id=?', [id]);
  await redis.incr(`product:ver:${id}`); // invalidates current cache
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness under concurrent reads/writes and ensure no stale data leaks?
- What are the trade-offs of using Redis vs Memcached for per-item versioned caches?
